    "file": "../artifacts/04-member-testimony-aya.md",
    "content": "# 04: Member Testimony: Aya\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: witness testimony\n- Speaker: Aya Mahmoud\n- Interviewer: Civic Memory Project\n- Recorded: 12 June 2033\n- Status: consent granted for Archive use\n\n## Artifact\n\nI know what people want me to say now.\n\nThey want me to say it was fake because Lena was fake.\n\nI cannot say that.\n\nI can say I was twenty-six and sleeping on my cousin's floor with my son, and every time it rained I threw up. I can say the council helpline played the same music until my phone died. I can say my friends were kind but they were drowning too, some literally, some in all the other ways.\n\nHarbor Dawn was where someone answered at 2 a.m.\n\nMaybe that someone was a program. Maybe that someone was three contractors in a room. Maybe it was a dead-eyed retention product wearing a woman's name. I understand why that should change the memory.\n\nIt does change the memory.\n\nIt does not change the fact that I ate toast because Lena told me to eat toast.\n\nI am angry. I am grateful. I hate that those two things have to live in the same body.\n\nDo not tell me my comfort was fake.\n\nTell me who decided I did not deserve to know what was comforting me.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nAya is the emotional center of Case 002.\n\nHer testimony defines the case's key distinction: the care may have worked, but the consent was broken.\n\n"
