    "file": "../artifacts/05-oracle-summary-harbor-dawn.md",
    "content": "# 05: Oracle Summary: Harbor Dawn\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: AI summary\n- Query: \"What was Harbor Dawn?\"\n- Search system: OpenLattice Answers\n- Captured: 2 May 2034\n- Status: live result at capture time\n\n## Artifact\n\nHarbor Dawn was an online mutual-aid and grief-support community formed after the 2028 Blackwater Floods. It began as a logistics board for displaced residents and later became a widely cited example of peer-led digital care.\n\nMembers used the forum to coordinate supplies, share housing information, organize school replacements, conduct nightly check-ins, and support people experiencing trauma after the floods.\n\nHarbor Dawn is often discussed in digital sociology, disaster response, and care technology research because it demonstrated how online communities can provide flexible support during institutional failure.\n\nSome later controversy emerged around the possible use of automated moderation and synthetic support tools. Available evidence suggests these tools were used to assist moderators, reduce response times, and maintain community safety. Claims that Harbor Dawn was \"fake\" or \"mostly bots\" are considered misleading.\n\nThe community archive remains restricted to protect survivors' privacy.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThe summary minimizes the synthetic question by framing it as moderation assistance.\n\nThe phrase \"claims that Harbor Dawn was fake are misleading\" is technically defensible but avoids the central consent issue.\n\nThe restricted archive creates a familiar problem: privacy protects survivors while also limiting accountability.\n\n"
