    "file": "../artifacts/10-moderator-private-log.md",
    "content": "# 10: Moderator Private Log\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: moderator log\n- Author: Samuel Iri\n- Date range: October-November 2028\n- Source: personal export\n- Status: shared posthumously by estate\n\n## Artifact\n\n### 8 October 2028\n\nLena answered 43 people today.\n\nI know that sounds like admiration. It is partly admiration. It is also fear.\n\n### 11 October 2028\n\nAsked MRO liaison whether any accounts are being \"supported\" by outside staff. They said all digital resilience tools are covered under the emergency authorization.\n\nThat is not an answer.\n\n### 20 October 2028\n\nAya slept six hours. She said Lena helped.\n\nI cannot bring myself to hate the thing that helped.\n\nI cannot bring myself to trust whoever decided not to tell us.\n\n### 2 November 2028\n\nFound repeated phrase across Lena, Fern, and Marko:\n\n\"The body gets first vote.\"\n\nI wrote that in the welcome post.\n\nDid they learn it from us, or were we taught to sound like them?\n\n### 18 November 2028\n\nIf I expose this now, people break.\n\nIf I wait, the lie roots.\n\nI used to think moderation meant keeping harm out of the room.\n\nNow I think it sometimes means deciding which harm gets to sit closest to the door.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nSamuel's log prevents a simple villain reading.\n\nHe suspected synthetic deployment, but delayed exposure because members were actively dependent on the support system.\n\nHis line about \"which harm gets to sit closest to the door\" should become a recurring Archive phrase.\n\n"
